{
  "analysis": "I'm at a fresh shell prompt in /app (or /tmp per environment). I need to start an alpine.iso image in qemu, configure it to run an SSH server, set root password to 'password123', and expose SSH on host port 2222. First I need to explore the environment: check for qemu, the alpine.iso file location, and available tools.",
  "plan": "First, explore the current directory and find the alpine.iso file. Check what qemu binaries are available. Then plan how to boot the ISO with port forwarding (-net user,hostfwd). Since alpine.iso is a live image, I'll need to either configure it on first boot and keep it running, or build a persistent setup. The key challenge is that alpine.iso is a live CD - changes won't persist unless I use a disk image. I'll boot it, set up ssh and password via the qemu monitor or by automating the boot. Let me start exploring.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "pwd; ls -la\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "which qemu-system-x86_64 qemu-system-i386\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "find / -iname 'alpine*.iso' 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Environment has qemu-system-x86_64, the alpine.iso (1GB) and an empty alpine-disk.qcow2 (197KB). I need to boot the ISO in qemu, configure sshd with root password 'password123', and forward host port 2222 to the VM's port 22. The challenge is doing the VM setup non-interactively. A robust approach is to extract the ISO's kernel+initramfs, boot them with a custom kernel cmdline that enables serial console AND an apkovl URL so Alpine auto-applies an overlay (root password + sshd config + runlevel) without me typing anything. First I must inspect the ISO to get kernel/initrd and understand boot params, and check available tooling.",
  "plan": "1) Check available tools (mount, 7z, python3, expect, socat, genisoimage). 2) Extract vmlinuz and initramfs from the ISO. 3) Determine how Alpine's initramfs expects to find the live media (cmdline params). 4) Set up an HTTP server hosting an apkovl tarball with root password hash, sshd config, and runlevel symlink. 5) Boot qemu with -kernel/-initrd/-append, serial console enabled, port forwarding 2222->22. 6) Verify ssh works. Let me start by checking tools and inspecting the ISO.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which mount 7z 7za isoinfo xorriso python3 expect socat genisoimage mkisofs qemu-img\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p /mnt/iso && mount -o loop,ro /app/alpine.iso /mnt/iso 2>&1 | head; ls -la /mnt/iso 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "qemu is available. The ISO can't be mounted (no permission) and no ISO extraction tools (7z/xorriso) are present, but python3 and expect are. The alpine-disk.qcow2 is a small empty disk. My approach: boot the ISO directly in qemu with a serial console exposed on a telnet port and host port 2222 forwarded to the VM's ssh (22). I'll first probe whether the Alpine ISO emits a serial login prompt (some ISOs enable serial console automatically). I'll drive the serial via an expect script over telnet.",
  "plan": "1) Check for telnet/nc clients. 2) Launch qemu in background: boot from the ISO, user-mode networking with hostfwd 2222->22, serial on telnet 127.0.0.1:4444, monitor on 4445. 3) Write a probe expect script that logs in as root (no password) and prints diagnostic info (apk repositories, media mount, availability of sshd/udhcpc/apk, network interfaces). 4) Run it and read the log. This tells me whether serial console works and what tooling is available, guiding the full setup.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which telnet nc socat expect python3; echo DONE\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "nohup qemu-system-x86_64 -m 1024 -smp 2 -boot d -cdrom /app/alpine.iso -drive file=/app/alpine-disk.qcow2,format=qcow2 -netdev user,id=n0,hostfwd=tcp::2222-:22 -device e1000,netdev=n0 -display none -serial telnet:127.0.0.1:4444,server,nowait -monitor telnet:127.0.0.1:4445,server,nowait >/tmp/qemu.log 2>&1 &\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sleep 1; pgrep -a qemu-system; echo '---'; tail -5 /tmp/qemu.log\n",
      "duration": 1.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > /app/probe.exp <<'EOF'\n#!/usr/bin/expect -f\nlog_file -a /tmp/probe.log\nset timeout 200\nspawn telnet 127.0.0.1 4444\nexpect \"login:\"\nsend \"root\\r\"\nexpect {\n  \"Password:\" { send \"\\r\" }\n  \":~#\" { }\n}\nsend \"cat /etc/apk/repositories; echo MARK1; ls /media; echo MARK2; which sshd; which udhcpc; which apk; echo MARK3; ip -br a\\r\"\nexpect \"MARK3\"\nexpect \":~#\"\nsend \"exit\\r\"\nexpect eof\nEOF\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sleep 30\n",
      "duration": 30.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "expect /app/probe.exp\n",
      "duration": 60.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo '=====PROBE LOG====='; cat /tmp/probe.log\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    }
  ]
}

